"""render_benchmark_data.py — regenerate web/src/underhood/benchmarkData.ts
from the real docmeta P0 evaluation-harness run (backlog O31).

SOURCE OF TRUTH for the numbers this script emits:
``knowledge/upgrade-plans/p0-benchmark/benchmark_p0_results.json`` — the
committed, raw mechanical output of
``knowledge/upgrade-plans/p0-benchmark/benchmark_p0.py``'s 2026-07-16 live
run against the ``drydocs`` DB (see ``docmeta-p0-verdict.md``'s Setup table
for corpus/run provenance). That JSON *is* the "real evaluation-harness run"
named in O31's acceptance. Before this script existed, ``benchmarkData.ts``
said so about itself: its own header called every number "a verbatim
transcription of the 2026-07-16 live benchmark verdict" — i.e. hand-retyped
from ``docmeta-p0-verdict.md``'s prose tables, not read from the JSON. That
hand-retyping produced at least one real bug this script fixes by
construction: the verdict's own Results table lists six full-text ``✔``
marks (EL1, EL3, PC2, PC3, MD1, MD2) but its "Recall" summary row says
"7/12" — an arithmetic slip that was then copied into benchmarkData.ts's
``TOTALS.fulltext.recall`` unchanged. Because this script *computes*
TOTALS/EFFICIENCY/STRATEGIES.recall from the same per-question table it
emits (never a second hand-typed copy), that class of drift is now
structurally impossible.

WHY "kind" (pass/fail/partial/abstain/hallucination) is a cited table below,
not a formula over ``marker_found``:
the benchmark's regex markers are a cheap proxy and are demonstrably wrong
in both directions on this dataset — proof, not assertion:
  * SA1/SA2 full-text: marker_found=True (the returned chunks happen to
    contain the literal substrings "controlm-"/"SYNTHESIZED"), but neither
    question was actually answered — full-text cannot aggregate, and the
    verdict correctly scores both ``fail`` ("no aggregation").
  * MD1/PV1 traversal: marker_found=False (the Cypher answer is a doc list /
    per-tier count, not prose that echoes the marker literal), but the
    answers are objectively correct — the verdict's own text names this
    exact correction ("MD1 and PV1 traversal queries returned correct
    answers whose output columns didn't echo the marker literal").
A script that trusted ``marker_found`` as ground truth would silently get
both of these wrong. So ``ADJUDICATED_KIND`` below is a literal, cited
transcription of docmeta-p0-verdict.md's Results table (the human/LLM-judge
call the verdict discloses it made — "Judge = the author"), and everything
*downstream* of that table (totals, recall fractions, the efficiency
multiplier, the per-arm chars/latency shown on the strategy cards) is
computed, not retyped.

What IS computed straight from the JSON, mechanically, with no table:
  * chars / ms per (question, arm) — read directly off benchmark_p0_results.json.
  * the CORPUS provenance-tier breakdown (GROUNDED/SYNTHESIZED/VERBATIM
    chunks+chars) — parsed out of SA2's own recorded ``sample`` field,
    which is the full JSON blob the traversal arm returned (179 chars,
    under the script's 300-char sample cap, so nothing is truncated).
  * TOTALS (chars sum, recall fraction, token estimate, median latency),
    EFFICIENCY (headline multiplier + per-question min/max ratio), and the
    STRATEGIES card recall/tokens/latency strings — all derived from the
    per-question table this script also emits, so they cannot drift from it.

What is NOT recoverable from the JSON at all, and stays a cited constant:
  * CORPUS.docCount / totalChars / chunkCount — the benchmark script never
    counts total corpus docs/chars/chunks; that's a *load-time* fact
    (``docmeta-p0-verdict.md``'s Setup table: "26 controlm-*.md, 313,350
    chars ... 374 chunks"). See the NOTE beside CORPUS_DOC_COUNT below for a
    live discrepancy this script noticed but does not silently resolve.
  * The prose (STRATEGIES.how/failureModes, per-question .note, .cypher
    snippets) — narrative content authored for the verdict/explainer docs,
    not benchmark output. Kept as cited constants; QUESTION_PROSE below
    names its source per block.

Usage:
    poetry run python scripts/render_benchmark_data.py

Drift guard: tests/unit/test_benchmark_data_ts.py re-runs this module's
``build_module_source()`` and asserts it matches the committed .ts byte for
byte (the ``test_software_registry_json.py`` / gates.json pattern, adapted
for a .ts target instead of a .json target since there is no separate
consumer-side loader to diff against — the committed .ts *is* the artifact).
"""

